{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.HabiroRing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.HabiroRing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.algebraicVolumeHabiro","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.algebraicVolumeHabiro","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L71-L74","doc":"The algebraic volume embeds into the Habiro ring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.algebraicVolume","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.algebraicVolume","kind":"def","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L66-L69","doc":"The algebraic quantum volume lives in the Habiro ring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.embed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.embed","kind":"def","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L61-L64","doc":"Laurent polynomials embed into the Habiro ring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement.compatible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement.compatible","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L59-L59","doc":"Compatibility: at level n, agrees with level n+1 modulo (q;q)_n. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement.family","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement.family","kind":"def","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L57-L57","doc":"Representatives at each level. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement.mk","kind":"ctor","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L55-L55","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroElement","kind":"structure","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L52-L59","doc":"The Habiro ring is the inverse limit of these quotients.\nAn element is a compatible family of equivalence classes. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:def:habiro-ring","label":"def:habiro-ring","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.HabiroEquiv","kind":"def","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L44-L50","doc":"The quotient ring Z[q] / ((q;q)_n) as an equivalence relation.\nTwo polynomials are equivalent modulo (q;q)_n if their\ndifference is divisible by (1-q)(1-q²)...(1-qⁿ). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.HabiroRing.qPochhammer","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.HabiroRing.qPochhammer","kind":"def","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-ring.lean#L39-L42","doc":"The q-Pochhammer symbol (q;q)_n = (1-q)(1-q²)...(1-qⁿ). ","signatureChunk":0}]}